    "result_text": "Done. The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city.\n\n**`PostcodeRange.java`** \u2014 added `regionForPostcode(String)`, the reverse of the region-to-range table it already owns. It returns the region whose inclusive range contains the postcode, or `null` when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range. This keeps the range knowledge in the one class that already holds it.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.locality(Owner)`** \u2014 the DTO derivation now coordinates the two lookups: postcode first, then the existing `CityRegion` city table as fallback:\n\n```java\ndefault String locality(Owner owner) {\n    String byPostcode = PostcodeRange.regionForPostcode(owner.getPostcode());\n    return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : CityRegion.locality(owner.getCity());\n}\n```\n\nKnown cities still resolve to the same region (their pinned postcodes fall in-range), while a postcode disambiguates cities sharing a name \u2014 e.g. an unknown city with a `3000` postcode now yields `VIC`. No logic was copied: `CityRegion` still owns the city table, `PostcodeRange` owns the ranges, and the mapper method remains the small single-responsibility derivation unit.",
